<speak>
<p>We know that Delta of f is a root of unity, with residue one above every prime dividing u. First isolate the general local argument. In a local domain with residue characteristic p, let xi be a root of unity with residue one. Write its order as p to the a times m, where p does not divide m. Raise xi to p to the a, and call the result eta. Its order is m, and its residue is still one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Suppose m is greater than one, so eta is not one. The geometric identity factors the difference between eta to the m and one as eta minus one times the sum of its first m powers, starting at one. Eta to the m is one. Since we are in a domain and eta minus one is nonzero, that sum must vanish.<break time="700ms"/>The closed polygon illustrates this exact sum in the complex plane. Its arrows are separate from the local residue calculation.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Now reduce the vanishing sum in the residue field. Each of its m terms becomes one. Their sum is the residue of the integer m, which is nonzero because p does not divide m. This contradicts the vanishing sum. Therefore m is one: the original root has p-power order. Order one is allowed.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Apply this to Delta of f, with u odd and greater than one. For every prime p dividing u, we have residue one at a prime above p. Such primes exist: the cyclotomic ring is a nonzero free abelian group of finite rank, so its quotient by p is nonzero. The local argument makes the order of Delta a power of p. Since every prime dividing u is odd, the order is odd.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>If u has two distinct prime divisors p and q, the order is a power of both. The only common positive integer is one, so Delta equals one in that case. With only one prime divisor, a nontrivial rotation can remain. Odd order is the conclusion that covers both cases.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For u equal to three, Delta lies in the field generated by i and omega, of degree four over the rationals. Its order is a power of three. A primitive ninth root has degree six, so it cannot lie in this field. Higher powers of three require still larger degrees. Only three directions remain: one, omega, and omega squared.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For the actual norm example f of P equals three i P, f of one is three i, and f of omega is three i omega. Their quotient is omega inverse, or omega squared. The phase therefore need not be zero. Applying the theorem to c, d, and g gives three odd-order phases. Node six will combine that arithmetic fact with their common sign origin.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
